  "_meta": {
    "accent": "southern",
    "hints": "Founder-editable drafts aligned with the Vietnamese alphabet guide (2026-10-23). Pieces use \" - \"; syllables within a word use spaces. Internal hyphens mark a glide within one syllable, never an extra beat. Initial d/gi/v use y; đ uses d; r stays r; tr stays tr; Southern qu uses w. Initial th uses th for an aspirated t, never English th. E/ê use e in closed hints and eh in open hints, as em/met and feh; i/y use ee, or i before Southern n/t endings. A/ă use ah (short for ă); o uses aw; ô uses oh; â/ơ/ư use uh with the guide’s distinct mouth cues. Clusters follow the guide examples: tiền teeyen, muốn mwohng, chưa chuah, nước nuuhk, được duhk, đâu doh, đói daw-ee, theo theh-oh. Iêu uses ee-uh-oo by composition; uyê uses weeye; uôi uses wohy. Ai/ay retain the guide’s igh/ye/y cues; oi/ôi use oy except the explicit đói example; ơi/ưi/ây use uhy; ui uses ooy; ao/au use ow. Ng is the end of sing even initially; ny is canyon’s joined sound; kh is back-of-mouth friction. Southern back/central-vowel n/t use ng/k; front-vowel endings use n/t, including inh/ich and anh/ach. Final p/t/k close without release or added vowel. Every non-level syllable has its lexical tone mark: ↑ sắc, ↓ huyền, ⌄ merged Southern hỏi/ngã, • nặng; no mark is ngang. Broad hints do not replace the selected voice’s audio.",
    "sound_reference": "General notation background only (not the target accent): James P. Kirby (2011), Vietnamese (Hanoi Vietnamese), https://doi.org/10.1017/S0025100311000181; southern vowel and consonant comparison: Andrea Hoa Pham (2009), https://people.clas.ufl.edu/apham/files/Pham-2009-The-identity-of-non-identified-sounds-glottal-stop-prevocalic-w-and-tripthongs-in-Vietnamese.pdf"
  }
